The `env_0037_residential_hvac_company` scenario is finished. All 5 difficulties pass the policy audit and the knowledge-base audit, and `validate_scenario` returns **VALID** (5 difficulties, 10 tasks, all 10 loading, 56 tools, 346 documents).

**What was built** (company: Harbison Heating & Air, Columbus OH)
- **Blueprint:** 7 product lines with 6 tiers each, a 25-table database and shared lookup tools.
- **5 difficulties, 2 tasks each:**

| Difficulty | Product | Rework rounds before passing |
|---|---|---|
| `comfort_club_membership_care` | maintenance plans (`plan`) | 1 |
| `protection_contract_claims` | protection contracts (`warranty`) | 3 |
| `repair_and_emergency_service` | service calls (`service`) | 2 |
| `replacement_installation_projects` | installations (`install`) | 2 |
| `home_comfort_financing_servicing` | financing (`finance`) | 1 |

- **Knowledge base:** 346 documents across all 7 products, every document check green.
  - The 5 task products were audited by fresh agents; each audit worked out the answers from only what a live agent can see, and they matched the answer keys exactly.
  - The 2 products no task uses, `filter` (15 docs) and `lease` (16 docs), were never audited, as the pipeline intends; only the document check stands behind them.

**What the policy audits caught and how it was fixed**
- Adjustments labelled as changing the calculation method that were really just multipliers. They were relabelled, and each calculation got a real method change.
- Base rules overridden without saying so, and rule text that disagreed with the calculation.
- A prerequisite that was already met when the call opens.
- A change order whose scope wasn't on any record. The customer now says what it was for when asked.

**Decisions I made on my own**
- **Protection contracts:** after the third rework both tasks passed. The auditor still had one realism issue in the maintenance-history rule, so I sent it back once more given your realism priority. That left a narrower edge case (N-1″) which no task triggers and which no scored value depends on; the auditor traced it to the formula language having no way to express "latest record before a date". I accepted the difficulty as passing rather than keep looping.
- **Base-layer wording:** a few parameter descriptions in the frozen blueprint still describe rules that later overrides retired, such as service "0 means due on completion" and the install deposit "percent of the contract price". Per the base-freeze rule they are published as written, and no scored value depends on them.
- **Same-product gaps:** some override rules apply to fewer tiers or cases than the base rules they retire, leaving some cases with no rule in force. For example, ordinary Comfort Club dues pricing (outside reinstatement) has none, and some warranty tiers have no refund or transfer basis. No task reaches these.

These are logged as `BLUEPRINT-KNOWN` / `KNOWN` entries in `env/env_0037_residential_hvac_company/worklog.md`, which records every launch, cut-off, rework and verdict.

